'''Social metabolism''' (or societal metabolism) examines the flows of materials and energy between human societies and their environments. It includes extraction, production, consumption, accumulation and waste.

== Intellectual history ==
Fischer-Kowalski's 1998 review traces applications of metabolic thinking to societies and material-flow analysis. Fischer-Kowalski and Hüttler survey research across scales and flow types. Modern societal-metabolism analysis specifies a socioeconomic system boundary rather than treating society literally as a biological cell.

== Material and energy accounting ==
For a fixed material and consistent time interval, mass balance gives <math>\Delta S=I-O</math>, where S is stock and I and O are boundary inputs and outputs. If 100 tonnes enter and 75 tonnes leave, stock increases by 25 tonnes, assuming the accounting includes every relevant flow and transformation for the defined conserved quantity. Economy-wide accounts distinguish domestic extraction, imports, exports and changes in stock. Energy accounting requires explicit units and boundaries; energy quality and conversion efficiency cannot be inferred from total mass.

== Applications and limits ==
Material-flow analysis, life-cycle assessment and input–output models answer different questions. Territorial accounting can miss impacts embodied in imports, while consumption-based accounting depends on supply-chain data. A reduction in material use per unit of output does not guarantee reduction in absolute consumption. Document uncertainty, stock lifetimes and classification rules. Physical throughput does not by itself explain social institutions, inequality or environmental justice.

